on practice in maintained OSS projects.'","AC: 'The resulting evidence cites maintained OSS concurrency-regression precedents.'","Retrieved prior art directly on the plan's actual method: Koochakzadeh & Garousi (2010), 'A Tester-Assisted Methodology for Test Redundancy Detection' (hindawi.com/journals/ase/2010/932686) — uses manually injected faults/mutants to evaluate redundancy detection; broader mutation-testing literature frames injecting defects to assess whether tests distinguish behavior.","The plan's prior-art aim (concurrency-regression practice) validates the SUBJECT under test but not the CONSOLIDATION-VIA-FAULT-SEEDING technique that is the plan's central act."],"finding":"The plan's prior-art comparison is scoped to 'concurrency-regression practice in maintained OSS projects', but the technique actually being applied is fault-seeded equivalence proof (independently seeded defects run against two oracles to build a detection matrix before removing a test) — a well-established test-redundancy-detection / mutation-testing method. The plan cites OSS concurrency precedent but does not anchor its core consolidation METHOD in that established methodology, leaving the strongest supporting prior art unstated.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f25c645c8ff316afc","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan > final bullet ('compare concurrency-regression practice in maintained OSS projects') and Acceptance Criteria line 'cites maintained OSS concurrency-regression precedents'","norm_id":"n3056ec517b6ac46b","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer or future maintainer questions whether 'runtime overlap does not prove redundancy' is a sound basis for removing a regression test — the fault-seeded-equivalence method is standard, but the plan does not point to the literature that establishes it.","The detection matrix is built but its methodology is treated as bespoke rather than an instance of a known, validated ap